# Rendering on the Quest with passthrough

Written October 21, 2026, after two headset builds failed on compositing and look. The facts below are what every headset work order has to respect. `.claude/skills/quest-mr-rendering/SKILL.md` points the orchestrator here; work orders list this file under Read.

## How passthrough reaches the eye

1. The Passthrough block's `OVRPassthroughLayer` is an underlay: the compositor draws the cameras' image behind the app's eye buffer and uses the eye buffer's alpha to blend. Alpha 0 shows the room, alpha 1 shows the app, values between blend.
2. The eye buffer uses premultiplied alpha (`OVRManager.eyeFovPremultipliedAlphaModeEnabled`, on by default). A pixel that should show the room is (0, 0, 0, 0). A half-transparent pixel is (rgb × a, a). Never write colour with alpha 0 and never disable premultiplied alpha (the Real Hands block does; that is why it is out, K34).
3. Everything that touches the alpha channel matters:
   - The XR camera clears to Solid Color with alpha 0. A Skybox clear writes alpha 1 everywhere and hides the room.
   - HDR off on the camera and on the Mobile render pipeline asset. The 11-11-10 HDR colour format has no alpha channel; with it the whole view goes opaque.
   - Post-processing off on the XR camera (and no Volume with Vignette, Bloom or Tonemapping in the scene). URP's post-processing writes alpha 1 unless Alpha Processing is on in the pipeline asset; off is simpler and cheaper.
   - Opaque shaders write alpha 1, which is correct for the full-scale site once it is fully there. Transparent shaders with `Blend SrcAlpha OneMinusSrcAlpha` leave a sensible alpha behind.
   - MSAA resolves alpha normally. Render scale and upscaling keep alpha.
4. Anything meant to show the room must not be drawn at all (`clip()` or not rendered); do not cover the view with a mesh that writes alpha 0, because any mistake in that mesh (position, stuck animation, culling) cuts the view into a window. The October 21 build did this with a 4 m sphere around the head and the player saw the site through a hole.
5. The sky in the site is a dome mesh with its own shader, never the camera skybox, so it can fade in and out like the rest of the site.
6. Turn the passthrough layer off (`enabled = false`, `hidden = true`) only when the site is fully opaque, and on again before anything starts to dissolve. While it is on, the compositor pays for it; while it is off, alpha does nothing.

## Stereo and shaders

- Single-pass instanced rendering. Every hand-written shader has `#pragma multi_compile_instancing`, `UNITY_VERTEX_INPUT_INSTANCE_ID`, `UNITY_VERTEX_OUTPUT_STEREO`, `UNITY_SETUP_INSTANCE_ID`, `UNITY_INITIALIZE_VERTEX_OUTPUT_STEREO` and `UNITY_SETUP_STEREO_EYE_INDEX_POST_VERTEX`, and uses URP's `Core.hlsl`, `Lighting.hlsl` and `Shadows.hlsl` transforms, never `UNITY_MATRIX_VP` by hand.
- Lit shaders go through `UniversalFragmentPBR` (or `UniversalFragmentBlinnPhong` where cheaper) with `GetMainLight(shadowCoord)` so they receive the directional shadows and the SH ambient, and `MixFog` for the fog.
- Global values for an effect over the whole site go through `Shader.SetGlobalFloat` and `Shader.SetGlobalVector`, read by every site shader; no per-material loops in C#.
- Vulkan on the Quest compiles `clip()` fine. Avoid dependent texture reads in loops, `discard` in a depth prepass (there is none) and more than three texture samples per layer.
- Shader Graph is allowed when a graph is easier to keep than HLSL, with the same stereo rules (Shader Graph handles them), alpha clip on, and the warp values as global properties (exposed off).

## Budget for the Quest 3S at 72 Hz

- About 300k visible triangles, 150 draw calls (static batching on for the builder's static geometry, GPU instancing on materials for repeated props).
- One directional light with soft shadows, one cascade, 2048 shadow map, shadow distance 60 m. No point or spot lights.
- MSAA 4x (cheap on a tile GPU). Render scale 1.0 (0.8 blurs text and distant detail). Fixed foveated rendering Low; Medium and High blur the edge of the view enough that testers call it a lens.
- Ambient from a gradient (trilight) or a baked sky probe. Linear fog from 300 m to 2,500 m into the horizon colour; it is what gives the flat ground depth.
- No screen-space ambient occlusion, no post-processing, no realtime reflection probes.
- Textures 1024² or 2048², ASTC 6x6; normal maps on every ground and prop material, because flat colour without normals is what reads as a drawing.

## Readable and comfortable

- Text at 0.5 m: cap height at least 1.5 cm (2.5 cm for titles), TextMeshPro SDF, high contrast on an opaque panel. Buttons at least 11 by 6 cm.
- Nothing moves the player: the world changes in place (the site root is moved before it appears; the rig and the camera are never moved). Transitions last 1 to 2 s. No vignette is needed because there is no vection.
- A transition always finishes: a watchdog snaps it to its end state after 3 s, Back is never blocked, and each phase logs one line (`adb logcat -s Unity`) so a stuck state can be read from the device.

## Seeing the real thing before a build

The preview camera uses identity local pose and the centre eye camera's clipping planes as well as its field of view; the separate, explicitly named SurveyCamera is only for WO-L4 statistics, never acceptance images.

Every acceptance screenshot is rendered through the rig's centre eye: a camera parented to the centre eye anchor with identity local pose and the centre eye camera's field of view, so anything attached to the head (the Interaction SDK's tunneling quads, masks, panels) appears exactly as the headset shows it; a free preview camera saw the tunnel quads edge-on and three builds shipped with a grey circle (K42, K43). The Interactions Rig's `SmoothMovementTunneling` and `WallPenetrationTunneling` objects are inactive in every scene; Check Building Blocks fails while a `TunnelingEffect` renders.

The Editor cannot show passthrough. For every screenshot of a passthrough state, render the eye view to an ARGB32 texture and composite it over a stand-in room image (`Monitor/out/unity/room.png`, a mid-grey wall with a 20 cm grid) using the eye buffer's alpha; the result is what the compositor would show. Review those, not the raw frame. Quest Link with "Passthrough over Link" shows the real compositor in the headset but is not required.
# WO-L4 The site looks real

## Goal

Standing on the road in `Play.unity`, the headset player sees a construction site, not a drawing: textured ground with relief, a real trench with spoil, bedding and pipe, a gravel road, fences, cones and barriers, the trailer, the crews and machines, a sky with sun and clouds, shadows, and haze toward the horizon, inside the Quest 3S budget of `Docs/XR_RENDERING.md`. Every site material uses one shader, `LOBOver/SiteLit`, so WO-L5 can add the warp to all of them at once. Bryan's words on the first build: the assets are extremely poor, it does not feel realistic, a horizontal line like a drawing.

## Read

- `AGENTS.md`, `Docs/XR_RENDERING.md` (all of it), `Docs/LEVEL1.md` ("In the headset"), `Docs/DECISIONS.md` K37 to K40
- `Assets/LOBOver/Editor/{PlaySceneBuilder,SiteAssetBuilder,SiteSceneBuilder,BuildingBlocksSetup}.cs`, `Assets/LOBOver/Runtime/World/**` (StationCell, CrewRig, SiteWorld, WorldAssets, WorldDrawing), `Assets/LOBOver/Runtime/Play/HostWorldPresenter.cs`, `Assets/LOBOver/Art/PlaySky.shader`, `Tools/import_lobgame_assets.ps1`
- LOB-Game's assets already imported under `Assets/LOBGame/` (the sand pile photoscan with its textures, `pipe/` with PBR textures, `road-barrier-collection`, `trafic-cone`, `water-barriers`, `Construction Assets/Traffic Props` fences and jersey barrier, the workers and machines) and the ones this order adds: `3D_Models/Tree` (`Tree 2/fast-urban-tree`, `Tree 2/beech`, `Tree1/tree`), `3D_Models/sophiaImports` (`trench_DigNFill_03.fbx` with its `groundTrenchDig` albedo, normal and metallic-smoothness textures), `3D_Models/traffic-props`, `3D_Models/construction-site-barrier`
- `Assets/Settings/Mobile_RPAsset.asset`, `Mobile_Renderer.asset`, `Assets/Oculus/OculusProjectConfig.asset`

## You may change

`Assets/LOBOver/Editor/**`, `Assets/LOBOver/Runtime/**`, `Assets/LOBOver/Art/**` (including generated textures and materials), `Assets/LOBOver/Tests/**`, `Assets/Settings/Mobile_RPAsset.asset` and `Mobile_Renderer.asset` (only the values named below), `Tools/import_lobgame_assets.ps1` (add folders only), `Play.unity` only through the builder. Never `Assets/LOBGame/**` (copies go to `Assets/LOBOver/Art/Converted/` as now), never a `[BuildingBlock]` object.

## Build

1. **Imports.** Add the four LOB-Game folders above to `Tools/import_lobgame_assets.ps1`; the orchestrator runs it before verification. Convert their Built-in materials to URP copies the way `SiteAssetBuilder` already does; every converted material ends on `LOBOver/SiteLit` (item 2) with its textures kept.
2. **`LOBOver/SiteLit`** (URP HLSL, `Art/SiteLit.shader`): opaque, lit through `UniversalFragmentPBR` with the main light and its shadows, SH ambient, fog; base map, normal map, smoothness and occlusion (a metallic-smoothness map where the source has one); tiling in world units for ground materials (triplanar for the ground mesh, so no stretching on slopes); a `_Splat` keyword variant for the ground that blends three tiled layers (dirt, gravel, dry grass) by vertex colour r, g, b; GPU instancing; the stereo macros of `XR_RENDERING.md`. One material per surface kind under `Art/Materials/`. The old `Soil`, `AccessRoad`, `Timber`, `StorageSteel`, `Repair_SiteCabin` copies move to this shader with the textures below.
3. **Textures.** If `Assets/` holds `TerrainLayer` assets or tileable textures whose names contain grass, dirt, soil, mud or gravel (a pack Bryan imported), use the best three for the ground layers and a dirt one for the trench. Otherwise generate them: an Editor generator writes tileable 1024² albedo, normal and smoothness textures to `Art/Generated/` for packed dirt (fBm with fine grain), gravel (Worley cells with varied stone tint), dry grass (streaked fBm, straw and olive), and a 256² soft dust sprite (radial falloff, brown, alpha) for the work particles. Normal maps from the height field; `sRGB` on albedo only; ASTC 6x6 on Android. LOB-Game's `groundTrenchDig` textures are the trench wall and bedding; the sand pile's albedo and occlusion are the spoil.
4. **Ground.** Replace the flat bank boxes with a built mesh, 1,200 m by 200 m, 2 m cells within 30 m of the trench and 10 m cells beyond (under 60,000 triangles), with gentle relief (fBm, 0.6 m amplitude, flat within 8 m of the trench and the road), vertex colours painting gravel on the road and its shoulders, dirt within 12 m of the trench, dry grass beyond, with noisy edges. A gravel road 4 m wide along the trench at `WorldUnits.RoadZ` with two shallow ruts. Beyond 100 m from the trench the ground continues as a simple plane to 3,000 m so the horizon never ends in a line against the sky. No colliders are needed on the site in Level 1 (the hub floor collider stays).
5. **Trench.** Per station cell, real geometry: a 1.2 m wide, 1.8 m deep cut with slightly irregular walls (noise-displaced), the trench wall texture; bedding gravel in the bottom when piped; LOB-Game's `PIPE.fbx` with its PBR textures as the laid pipe; a continuous spoil windrow on the far side from the road (noise-displaced mounds with the spoil texture, 1 m high, breaking every 30 m), the photoscanned sand pile as a hero pile at each crew's station; the slumped wall as a collapsed wedge with loose material on the floor; standing water as a dark reflective plane 0.3 m above the floor; seepage as a dark wet band on the lower wall; backfilled cells as a disturbed dirt strip at grade. Stakes: wooden lath 1.2 m, 5 cm wide, pink flagging, and a small station sign with the station text in 4 cm letters readable from the road.
6. **Props and surroundings.** Fences (`fence_long`) along the road's outer edge every 10 m for 60 m either side of each crew; cones and a barrier at the collapse and around each working machine; two jersey barriers at the gate; the trailer as a box with the site cabin material, a door and a window; a tree line (the lightest LOB-Game tree under 8,000 triangles, at most 30 instances, GPU instanced) 80 to 120 m beyond the far side with gaps; nothing within 20 m of the player that is a bare box.
7. **Sky and light.** A dome shader `LOBOver/SiteSky` (replaces `PlaySky`): if a cubemap skybox material exists under `Assets/` use its cubemap, else a procedural sky (zenith to horizon gradient, sun disc at the light's direction with a soft halo, two fBm cloud layers drifting slowly, a haze band at the horizon). The directional light: elevation 48°, warm white, soft shadows, one cascade, 2048, 60 m. Ambient trilight tuned to the sky. Linear fog 300 to 2,500 m in the horizon colour. Rain and the cloud bank keep working through `SiteSky`.
8. **Particles.** The work particles use the dust sprite on URP Particles/Unlit (alpha blended, soft, brown, small); no untextured white sprites anywhere.
9. **Settings** (values only): Mobile RP asset render scale 1.0, MSAA 4x, HDR off, no post-processing; `XRRuntimeSettings` foveation Low; static batching on the builder's static geometry; GPU instancing on repeated props.
10. **Scale sanity.** A worker is 1.8 m, the excavator cab roof about 3.2 m, the pipe 0.3 m outside diameter, the trench 1.2 m wide; the builder logs any imported model whose bounds are off by more than 25% and scales it.
11. **Tests.** EditMode: the builder runs twice and changes nothing the second time; every renderer under `[LOBOver] Site` uses `LOBOver/SiteLit`, `LOBOver/SiteSky` or URP Particles/Unlit; no material has a null main texture except the sky; the Editor stats at the pipe crew's station facing across the trench stay under 300k triangles and 150 draw calls (read through `UnityEditor.UnityStats`); the generated textures are tileable (the left and right edge columns match within 2%).

## Acceptance checks

- Orchestrator through the MCP: import script run; compile clean; Play Scene twice; tests pass; Play in the Editor with `Play LOB Over.cmd` running and the game started, at the pipe crew's station at eye height: screenshots facing across the trench, along the trench toward the excavator and toward the backfill crew, and one from the road at the collapse after Day 4, all through the preview camera at 1440 by 1440. Reviewed against the goal's first sentence: textured ground with relief, trench with spoil and pipe, road, fences and props, sky with clouds, shadows, haze, no white blobs, no bare boxes near the player; stats under budget.
- No build. Bryan builds and judges in the headset (K35, K41).

## Out of scope

The warp (WO-L5), the hub panel, voices, the Plan page.

## Amendment 1 (orchestrator, 2026-10-21 12:25)

Item 7, the sky: the procedural sky is the default. A cubemap skybox found under `Assets/` is used only when the sun probed from it sits above 30 degrees of elevation; then the light is aimed at it. In every other case the directional light keeps its 48 degree elevation and the procedural sky draws the sun disc at the light's direction with the cloud layers and the haze band. Reason: round 1 aimed the light at a daytime cubemap whose sun sits on the horizon, and the whole site went dark.

## Amendment 2 (orchestrator, 2026-10-22 00:05, from Bryan's round 3 authorization)

Two presentation requirements added to items 5 and 7. Item 7, the clouds: the two cloud layers fade out completely within 8 degrees of the horizon (zero cloud at and below 8 degrees of elevation, full strength again by about 16 degrees), and the cloud projection never stretches into radial streaks near the horizon; the haze band stays. Item 5, the trench walls: every trench surface (both walls, the slumped wall, the wet lower band, the floor) is textured by triplanar mapping in world units (about 1 m per tile) so the dirt texture reads continuous with no stretching or streaks, within the three-samples-per-layer rule of `Docs/XR_RENDERING.md`.

## Amendment 3 (orchestrator, 2026-10-22 02:00, measurement method for item 11)

Item 11's budget test measures through `UnityEditor.UnityStats`, which counts only what the Game view draws in a frame: an explicit `Camera.Render()` into a RenderTexture is never counted, and a hidden Scene view's repaint is. The test therefore: enables the preview camera with no target texture at the pipe crew's station facing across the trench, maximizes the Game view, disables every other camera and every renderer under the `[BuildingBlock] Camera Rig` for the measured frames (OVR hides those models on the device), combines the static-flagged site geometry with `StaticBatchingUtility.Combine` as the player build does, repaints the Game view, and reads `UnityStats` on the frame after the repaint, restoring everything afterwards. The assertions of item 11 apply to that frame only: under 300,000 triangles and 150 draw calls facing across the trench. The views along the trench (toward the excavator and toward the backfill crew) are logged with their triangle and draw-call figures and are not asserted. Reason: the orchestrator's frame-by-frame experiment (Monitor/out/unity/l4-framestats.txt) showed the delivered test reading the Scene view's repaint (1,031,536 triangles) instead of its own render; this amendment states the instrument, it does not change the budget.

## Amendment 4 (orchestrator, 2026-10-22 02:20, the measurement must not depend on the test runner's repaint cadence)

Under the MCP test runner (which switches the Editor to no throttling for the run) the Game view repaints twice within one `UnityStats` window, so the Amendment 3 instrument reads exactly twice the frame (305,376 triangles and 258 draw calls against 152,688 and 129 for the same frame under the Unity CLI runner). The test therefore counts the measured camera's renders inside the statistics window by subscribing to `RenderPipelineManager.beginCameraRendering` (counting only the measured camera) from the moment it enables the camera until it reads `UnityStats`, and divides the triangle and draw-call figures by that count (at least one) before asserting and logging; it unsubscribes in `finally`. Everything else in Amendment 3 stands.
# WO-L5 The site materializes around the player

## Goal

Touching a crew button in the hub builds the site around the player: a wave runs outward from the feet; just behind its front the ground and everything on it appear as glowing wire and blueprint lines over the room, then fill in with their real surfaces; the sky closes last. Back runs the wave inward and the room returns. No mask, no sphere, no window: the room is visible exactly where the site is not yet drawn, and once the site is complete the view is the whole site. Bryan's words on the first build: a fisheye with a lens; I can only see that part; it should be immersive going in and out.

## Read

- `AGENTS.md`, `Docs/XR_RENDERING.md` (all of it), `Docs/LEVEL1.md` ("In the headset"), WO-L4 (SiteLit, SiteSky), `Docs/DECISIONS.md` K37 to K41
- `Assets/LOBOver/Runtime/Play/{WarpController,HubPanel,HostWorldPresenter,PlayPreview}.cs`, `Assets/LOBOver/Editor/PlaySceneBuilder.cs`, `Assets/LOBOver/Art/{SiteLit,SiteSky}.shader`, `Assets/LOBOver/Tests/**`
- The headset log the orchestrator pulled from Bryan's test, if it exists (`Docs/WORK_ORDERS/logs/headset-20261021.log`), for the stuck transition

## You may change

`Assets/LOBOver/Runtime/**`, `Assets/LOBOver/Editor/**`, `Assets/LOBOver/Art/**`, `Assets/LOBOver/Tests/**`, `Play.unity` only through the builder.

## Build

1. **Global warp values.** `Shader.SetGlobalFloat("_WarpProgress")` (0 = no site, 1 = all of it), `SetGlobalVector("_WarpOrigin")` (the player's tracked feet in world space, y at the site's ground), `SetGlobalFloat("_WarpRadius")` (the wave front: 160 m × ease-out of progress), `SetGlobalFloat("_WarpBand")` (6 m). Set every frame during a transition, and once to the resting values (progress 1, radius 1e6) when the site is fully there.
2. **`SiteLit` with the warp.** In the fragment: `d = distance(positionWS, _WarpOrigin)`. `d > _WarpRadius + _WarpBand`: `clip(-1)` (nothing drawn; the room shows). Inside the band: the blueprint state, a dark translucent base (premultiplied: rgb 0.02, 0.04, 0.06 at alpha 0.25) with lines at alpha 1 in `#39C6FF`: on the builder's static meshes the lines are the triangle edges, from barycentric coordinates the builder bakes into UV3 (one vertex per corner; the ground, trench cells, road and spoil) with `fwidth`-based thickness of 1.5 px; on imported models (machines, workers, props, trees) a world-space 0.5 m grid (triplanar) plus a Fresnel rim in the same colour. From `d = _WarpRadius` inward over half the band the blueprint blends into the normal lit result (alpha 1). Alpha written exactly as the compositor needs it (premultiplied).
3. **`SiteSky` with the warp.** The dome fades in over progress 0.6 to 1.0 (premultiplied) and out in reverse, so the room stays visible overhead until the ground has mostly filled in. Fog colour follows it.
4. **`WarpController`.** Remove the sphere, the dissolve and fade materials and `Art/Warp.shader`, `Art/WarpFade.shader`. In: place the site root as now (road under the feet, trench ahead), enable the site, keep passthrough on, run progress 0 to 1 over 1.5 s with the ease above, then disable passthrough and hide the hub. Out: enable passthrough, run 1 to 0 over 1.2 s, disable the site, show the hub. Crews and animators freeze during the wave as now. A watchdog snaps any transition to its end state after 3 s of wall time. Back is never refused: pressed during a warp-in it reverses from the current progress; the hub's crew buttons are disabled only while a transition runs. Each phase logs one line, `LOBOver Warp: in start | in done | out start | out done | watchdog`, with the progress value, through `Debug.Log` so it reaches `adb logcat -s Unity`.
5. **Hub panel.** Unchanged in size and placement (WO-L2 item 4); during a transition its status line reads "Warping..." and the buttons grey out; on return it shows the day again.
6. **Preview composites.** `PlayPreview` gains a menu item `LOB Over > Debug > Warp Frames` that, in Play, steps progress to 0.2, 0.45, 0.7, 0.9 and 1.0, renders the preview camera to an ARGB32 texture at each, composites it over `Monitor/out/unity/room.png` (a generated mid-grey wall with a 20 cm grid; create it if missing) by the frame's alpha, and writes `Monitor/out/unity/warp-<progress>.png`. These are what the orchestrator reviews.
7. **Tests.** EditMode: the baked UV3 barycentrics sum to 1 on every vertex of the static meshes; no object in `Play.unity` carries the old sphere or the removed shaders; a source scan finds no `ZTest Always` and no full-view mesh in `Assets/LOBOver`. PlayMode against the Node host on port 7333: pressing Marcus sets progress from 0 to 1 within 1.5 s ± 0.1 and the global values follow; passthrough stays enabled until progress reaches 1 and is disabled after; the composite at progress 0.45 has alpha 0 pixels in its upper half and alpha 1 pixels near the bottom centre; Back at progress 0.5 reverses and ends with the site inactive and passthrough on; a transition whose coroutine is stopped ends by the watchdog within 3 s; the four log lines appear in order.

## Acceptance checks

- Orchestrator through the MCP: compile clean; Play Scene twice; tests pass; Warp Frames written and reviewed: the wave front is a ring on the ground around the player with wire and blueprint behind it, the room (grid wall) visible beyond it and overhead until late, the full site at 1.0 with no room left; no window, no mask. Console clean. No build.
- Bryan, after building: (1) the hub is the whole room with a readable panel; (2) Connected; (3) touching Marcus builds the site around him in about 1.5 s, wire first then surfaces, the full view, no lens; (4) Back takes the site away the same way; (5) the site looks like a site (WO-L4) and the voices play when the wall falls.

## Out of scope

The table model, the standee, stake-to-stake warps, Stop work, the Plan page.

## Amendment 1 (orchestrator, 2026-10-22 01:30, answer to BLOCKED)

Item 7, the source scan: `Assets/LOBOver/Runtime/VR/ComfortFade.cs` and `Assets/LOBOver/Art/ComfortFade.shader` (the legacy Site.unity inspector seat's eye-locked mask, compiled only under `UNITY_EDITOR || !UNITY_ANDROID`, never in `Play.unity`) are exempt from the scan; the scan covers the warp path and everything the builder puts into `Play.unity`. Leave the legacy files untouched (stake-to-stake travel is out of scope).
# WO-L7 No tunnel, head-camera verification, chart orientation

## Goal

The grey circle Bryan saw in three headset builds is gone for good, verification can never miss it again, and the Plan page's chart runs days across and stations up.

## The cause (found October 22)

The Interactions Rig block's `Locomotor` carries two Interaction SDK comfort effects, `SmoothMovementTunneling` and `WallPenetrationTunneling`. Each is a driver script in `Oculus.Interaction.Locomotion` plus a `TunnelingEffect` (namespace `Oculus.Interaction`) that draws a full-view quad 0.3 m in front of the centre eye (`ZTest Always`, transparent queue) with a feathered circular hole. `Configure Building Blocks` disables every Behaviour in `Oculus.Interaction.Locomotion`, so it switched off the drivers, which normally hide the quads every frame, but left the `TunnelingEffect` components running with their serialized defaults: the wall-penetration one shows a 60° hole in a grey (0.547) mask permanently. That is the "fisheye": the hub panel was hidden behind it except through the hole, and the site was visible only through the hole. Editor verification missed it three times because every screenshot came from a separate preview camera, which saw the quads edge-on as a "black plate" beside the foreman.

## Read

- `AGENTS.md`, `Docs/XR_RENDERING.md` (all of it), `Docs/DECISIONS.md` K42 and K43
- `Assets/LOBOver/Editor/BuildingBlocksSetup.cs`, `Assets/LOBOver/Runtime/Play/PlayPreview.cs`, `Assets/LOBOver/Editor/PlaySceneBuilder.cs` (the preview camera), `Assets/LOBOver/Tests/**`
- `Library/PackageCache/com.meta.xr.sdk.interaction*/Runtime/Scripts/Interaction/Models/Locomotion/Tunneling/*.cs`, `Runtime/Prefabs/Locomotion/{Locomotor,TunnelingEffect}.prefab`
- `Monitor/web/play.html` (the board), `Monitor/web/plan.html` (the chart), their e2e tests

## You may change

`Assets/LOBOver/Editor/**`, `Assets/LOBOver/Runtime/**`, `Assets/LOBOver/Tests/**`, `Play.unity` only through Configure and the builder; `Monitor/web/play.html`, `Monitor/web/plan.html`, `Monitor/e2e/**`, `Monitor/test/**`; `Docs/XR_RENDERING.md` only the lines named below.

## Build

1. **Configure Building Blocks** also sets inactive every GameObject under the blocks that carries a `TunnelingEffect` (`SetActive(false)`; values only, nothing added or removed), logging each. **Check Building Blocks** fails when any `TunnelingEffect` in the scene is on an active object with an enabled `MeshRenderer`, naming the object. If Bryan has already unchecked the two objects by hand, Configure changes nothing and Check passes.
2. **Verification camera.** The Editor preview renders through the head: the preview camera becomes a child of the rig's centre eye anchor with local pose identity, the centre eye camera's field of view and clipping planes, so anything attached to the head (tunnels, masks, panels) appears in every screenshot and composite exactly as the headset shows it. The old free preview pose is kept only as a second, explicitly named "survey" view for the WO-L4 stats, never for acceptance images. `XR_RENDERING.md` "Seeing the real thing before a build" gains one sentence saying so.
3. **Tests.** EditMode: a scene with an active `TunnelingEffect` fails Check and passes after Configure; Configure on Play.unity is idempotent. PlayMode: with the rig at a simulated head pose, a frame rendered through the head camera at progress 0 (hub) contains no pixel within 0.03 of the tunnel grey (0.547, 0.547, 0.547) and the composite over `room.png` shows the grid everywhere except the panel; a frame at warp progress 1 contains none either.
4. **Chart orientation**, `play.html` and `plan.html`: days across (left to right, Day 1 at the left), stations up (0+00 at the bottom, 30+00 at the top), zone bands horizontal, the day ruler vertical and sliding right, the PM's dashed plan and the inked actuals in the same crew colours. Labels: "Day" along the bottom, station marks along the left. The result stamp and the faint previous run follow. The e2e assertions on the board update accordingly.

## Acceptance checks

- Orchestrator through the MCP: compile clean; Check on the committed Play.unity reports the two tunnel objects (if Bryan has not already turned them off), Configure turns them off, Check passes, Play Scene twice; EditMode and PlayMode pass; the head-camera screenshots at the hub and in the site show no tunnel, no mask, no black plate; composites reviewed.
- `npm test` and the e2e pass; screenshots of the flipped board before Start, mid-run and at the result in `Monitor/out/play/`.
- No Quest build (K41). Bryan builds and checks: the whole room in the hub, the panel readable, the site filling in around him with no circle.

## Out of scope

Everything else in Level 1.

