# WO-L7 No tunnel, head-camera verification, chart orientation

## Goal

The grey circle Bryan saw in three headset builds is gone for good, verification can never miss it again, and the Plan page's chart runs days across and stations up.

## The cause (found October 22)

The Interactions Rig block's `Locomotor` carries two Interaction SDK comfort effects, `SmoothMovementTunneling` and `WallPenetrationTunneling`. Each is a driver script in `Oculus.Interaction.Locomotion` plus a `TunnelingEffect` (namespace `Oculus.Interaction`) that draws a full-view quad 0.3 m in front of the centre eye (`ZTest Always`, transparent queue) with a feathered circular hole. `Configure Building Blocks` disables every Behaviour in `Oculus.Interaction.Locomotion`, so it switched off the drivers, which normally hide the quads every frame, but left the `TunnelingEffect` components running with their serialized defaults: the wall-penetration one shows a 60° hole in a grey (0.547) mask permanently. That is the "fisheye": the hub panel was hidden behind it except through the hole, and the site was visible only through the hole. Editor verification missed it three times because every screenshot came from a separate preview camera, which saw the quads edge-on as a "black plate" beside the foreman.

## Read

- `AGENTS.md`, `Docs/XR_RENDERING.md` (all of it), `Docs/DECISIONS.md` K42 and K43
- `Assets/LOBOver/Editor/BuildingBlocksSetup.cs`, `Assets/LOBOver/Runtime/Play/PlayPreview.cs`, `Assets/LOBOver/Editor/PlaySceneBuilder.cs` (the preview camera), `Assets/LOBOver/Tests/**`
- `Library/PackageCache/com.meta.xr.sdk.interaction*/Runtime/Scripts/Interaction/Models/Locomotion/Tunneling/*.cs`, `Runtime/Prefabs/Locomotion/{Locomotor,TunnelingEffect}.prefab`
- `Monitor/web/play.html` (the board), `Monitor/web/plan.html` (the chart), their e2e tests

## You may change

`Assets/LOBOver/Editor/**`, `Assets/LOBOver/Runtime/**`, `Assets/LOBOver/Tests/**`, `Play.unity` only through Configure and the builder; `Monitor/web/play.html`, `Monitor/web/plan.html`, `Monitor/e2e/**`, `Monitor/test/**`; `Docs/XR_RENDERING.md` only the lines named below.

## Build

1. **Configure Building Blocks** also sets inactive every GameObject under the blocks that carries a `TunnelingEffect` (`SetActive(false)`; values only, nothing added or removed), logging each. **Check Building Blocks** fails when any `TunnelingEffect` in the scene is on an active object with an enabled `MeshRenderer`, naming the object. If Bryan has already unchecked the two objects by hand, Configure changes nothing and Check passes.
2. **Verification camera.** The Editor preview renders through the head: the preview camera becomes a child of the rig's centre eye anchor with local pose identity, the centre eye camera's field of view and clipping planes, so anything attached to the head (tunnels, masks, panels) appears in every screenshot and composite exactly as the headset shows it. The old free preview pose is kept only as a second, explicitly named "survey" view for the WO-L4 stats, never for acceptance images. `XR_RENDERING.md` "Seeing the real thing before a build" gains one sentence saying so.
3. **Tests.** EditMode: a scene with an active `TunnelingEffect` fails Check and passes after Configure; Configure on Play.unity is idempotent. PlayMode: with the rig at a simulated head pose, a frame rendered through the head camera at progress 0 (hub) contains no pixel within 0.03 of the tunnel grey (0.547, 0.547, 0.547) and the composite over `room.png` shows the grid everywhere except the panel; a frame at warp progress 1 contains none either.
4. **Chart orientation**, `play.html` and `plan.html`: days across (left to right, Day 1 at the left), stations up (0+00 at the bottom, 30+00 at the top), zone bands horizontal, the day ruler vertical and sliding right, the PM's dashed plan and the inked actuals in the same crew colours. Labels: "Day" along the bottom, station marks along the left. The result stamp and the faint previous run follow. The e2e assertions on the board update accordingly.

## Acceptance checks

- Orchestrator through the MCP: compile clean; Check on the committed Play.unity reports the two tunnel objects (if Bryan has not already turned them off), Configure turns them off, Check passes, Play Scene twice; EditMode and PlayMode pass; the head-camera screenshots at the hub and in the site show no tunnel, no mask, no black plate; composites reviewed.
- `npm test` and the e2e pass; screenshots of the flipped board before Start, mid-run and at the result in `Monitor/out/play/`.
- No Quest build (K41). Bryan builds and checks: the whole room in the hub, the panel readable, the site filling in around him with no circle.

## Out of scope

Everything else in Level 1.
## In the headset

Revised October 22, after the fourth build (the tunnel removed): one site, everything in front, real scale, words you can read, days you can see. Decisions K45 to K48; work order WO-L8.

- You start in your room, in full passthrough, and can see and talk to the others. Nothing is drawn but a panel within arm's reach: the day, the connection, and one button, Go to the site.
- Touch it. The site materializes around you where you stand: a wave runs outward from your feet; just behind its front everything is glowing wire and blueprint lines over the room, then the surfaces fill in, until the room is gone and the sky closes over you. You do not move; the site is built around you.
- You stand on the road. The trench runs across in front of you, 6 m away. The three crews work in front of you at real size: the pipe crew in the middle, the excavator to the right, the backfill loader to the left, nearer or farther as the real gaps between them change. Machines crawl as they work; you never move. The stake by each crew shows its real station. The rest of the site runs off to the horizon on both sides.
- Look at a crew for two seconds and you have walked over to it: its foreman greets you or says what is going on, and the host treats you as standing there. Whatever anyone says shows in a speech bubble over their head while it plays. A Log button on your wrist opens a panel in front of you with the last twenty lines, the day and the clock state; grab it and leave it anywhere.
- Each day the sun crosses the sky; at the day boundary the light dims to dusk and the new day opens with its number in front of you. When the wall falls you see it fall in front of you; when the foremen argue, they meet in front of you.
- Back on your left wrist dissolves the site into your room.

## Principles

1. **Claims, not conclusions.** A voice says what the speaker sees, feels or wants. It never names the situation code, the cause in manual terms, the days lost, the response options, or what to do. LOB-Game's PM event lines already work this way ("A trench wall came down. The pipe crew has stopped.").
2. **Every claim is checkable by another seat.** A foreman's number is a rate, a day or a station that Plan can find on the LOB or Rules can run through the front-page rule. A claim nobody can check is noise.
3. **Speakers are partial.** Each foreman argues for his crew and blames the other crew or the schedule. Both are partly right; the team reconciles them with the LOB and the manual.
4. **Problems hide in normal lines.** Deviations are never announced. The excavation foreman whose order never arrived sounds cheerful about a good digging day. Only a Site player who knows what the office ordered hears the problem, and only Plan can tell Site what was ordered.
5. **Each voice reaches one seat.** Foremen are heard in the headset only (or on `site.html`). The PM and the radio play on the Plan laptop; the PM also appears as the hub standee for the morning brief. Rules hears nothing; Rules has paper. Whatever matters crosses the table in someone's own words.
6. **Once only.** A line plays once. Listen near a crew replays that crew's last line. No transcript is shown during play; the end-of-stage change log lists who said what and when.
7. **No verdict voices.** No line responds to whether a decision was good. The PM pushes against holds, demobilizations and purchases whatever the outcome; the end line states facts.

using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Linq;
using LOBOver.Client;
using LOBOver.Play;
using LOBOver.World;
using LOBOver.XR;
using Meta.XR.BuildingBlocks;
using TMPro;
using UnityEditor;
using UnityEditor.SceneManagement;
using UnityEngine;
using UnityEngine.Rendering;
using UnityEngine.SceneManagement;
using Object = UnityEngine.Object;

namespace LOBOver.Editor
{
    public static class PlaySceneBuilder
    {
        public const string ScenePath = "Assets/Scenes/Play.unity"; // WO-L2 Amendment 1; Bryan owns this scene.
        private const float FloorSize = 20; // Existing tracked-floor guard, retained from WO-S6.
        [MenuItem("LOB Over/Build/Play Scene")]
        public static void BuildMenu()
        {
            if (!EditorSceneManager.SaveCurrentModifiedScenesIfUserWantsTo()) return;
            Build();
        }
        public static Scene OpenExisting()
        {
            if (!File.Exists(ScenePath)) throw new FileNotFoundException("Bryan must save the Building Blocks scene at " + ScenePath);
            var scene = SceneManager.GetSceneByPath(ScenePath);
            if (!scene.IsValid() || !scene.isLoaded) scene = EditorSceneManager.OpenScene(ScenePath, OpenSceneMode.Single);
            SceneManager.SetActiveScene(scene); return scene;
        }
        public static Scene Build(bool saveScene = true)
        {
            var scene = OpenExisting();
            if (!BuildingBlocksSetup.Check(scene)) throw new InvalidOperationException("Play Scene stopped: resolve the Building Blocks check first.");
            var replaced = scene.GetRootGameObjects().Where(r => r.name.StartsWith("[LOBOver]", StringComparison.Ordinal)).ToArray();
            foreach (var root in replaced)
                if (root.GetComponentInChildren<BuildingBlock>(true) != null || root.GetComponentInChildren<OVRCameraRig>(true) != null
                    || root.GetComponentsInChildren<Transform>(true).Any(t => t.name.StartsWith("[BuildingBlock]", StringComparison.Ordinal) || t.name == "OVRCameraRig"))
                    throw new InvalidOperationException("Play Scene stopped: protected block/rig under " + root.name + ". Nothing removed.");
            // WO-L4 owns new copies under Art; the legacy prefab directory is outside this order.
            var assets = SitePresentationBuilder.BuildAssets();
            var dark = Material("PlayPanel", "Universal Render Pipeline/Unlit", new Color(0.035f, 0.045f, 0.06f, 1));
            var buttonMaterial = Material("PlayButton", "Universal Render Pipeline/Unlit", new Color(0.18f, 0.22f, 0.28f, 1));
            foreach (var root in replaced) Object.DestroyImmediate(root);
            var lighting = Root("Lighting");
            var sun = WorldDrawing.Group("Sun", lighting).AddComponent<Light>(); sun.type = LightType.Directional;
            SitePresentationBuilder.Lighting(sun);
            foreach (var light in scene.GetRootGameObjects().SelectMany(r => r.GetComponentsInChildren<Light>(true)).Where(l => l != sun && l.enabled))
            {
                if (light.GetComponentInParent<BuildingBlock>(true) != null
                    || light.GetComponentsInParent<Transform>(true).Any(t => t.name.StartsWith("[BuildingBlock]", StringComparison.Ordinal)))
                    throw new InvalidOperationException("WO-L4: protected Building Block light would exceed the one-light budget: " + light.name);
                light.enabled = false; EditorUtility.SetDirty(light); PrefabUtility.RecordPrefabInstancePropertyModifications(light);
                Debug.Log("WO-L4: disabled extra scene light " + light.name + "; the site uses one directional sun.");
            }
            var hub = Root("Hub");
            var floor = WorldDrawing.Group("TrackedFloor", hub).AddComponent<BoxCollider>();
            floor.size = new Vector3(FloorSize, 0.1f, FloorSize); floor.center = new Vector3(0, -0.05f, 0);
            var site = Root("Site"); site.gameObject.SetActive(false);
            var sky = SitePresentationBuilder.Build(site, assets);
            var world = WorldDrawing.Group("SiteWorld", site).AddComponent<SiteWorld>(); world.assets = assets;
            world.sky = sky; world.stage = world.gameObject.AddComponent<StageLayout>(); world.stage.world = world;
            var client = Root("Client");
            var connection = client.gameObject.AddComponent<HostConnection>();
            var xr = client.gameObject.AddComponent<XRRig>(); client.gameObject.AddComponent<XRRuntimeSettings>();
            var presenter = client.gameObject.AddComponent<HostWorldPresenter>(); presenter.connection = connection; presenter.world = world;
            var warpRoot = Root("Warp"); var warp = warpRoot.gameObject.AddComponent<WarpController>();
            warp.connection = connection; warp.rig = xr; warp.presenter = presenter; warp.site = site; warp.hub = hub.gameObject;
            var panel = WorldDrawing.Group("CrewPanel", hub).transform;
            var background = WorldDrawing.Box("Background", panel, Vector3.zero, new Vector3(HubPanel.Width, 0.43f, 0.008f), dark);
            Object.DestroyImmediate(background.GetComponent<Collider>());
            Label("Title", panel, "LOB, Over", new Vector3(0, 0.17f, -0.008f), new Vector2(0.36f, 0.04f), HubPanel.TitleHeight, assets);
            var status = Label("Status", panel, "Looking for the game. Start it on the PC with Play LOB Over.",
                new Vector3(0, 0.09f, -0.008f), new Vector2(0.36f, 0.1f), HubPanel.TextHeight, assets);
            var hubPanel = client.gameObject.AddComponent<HubPanel>(); hubPanel.connection = connection; hubPanel.rig = xr; hubPanel.panel = panel; hubPanel.status = status;
            hubPanel.warp = warp;
            var click = SiteAssetBuilder.Required<AudioClip>(SiteAssetBuilder.Art + "Click.wav");
            var enter = Button(panel, "Go to the site", "", new Vector3(0, -0.02f, -0.012f),
                new Vector2(HubPanel.ButtonWidth, HubPanel.ButtonHeight), assets, buttonMaterial, click, warp);
            enter.name = "GoToSite"; enter.goToSite = true;
            var wrist = WorldDrawing.Group("BackWrist", warpRoot);
            var follower = wrist.AddComponent<RigAnchorFollower>(); follower.rig = xr; follower.leftWrist = true;
            follower.positionOffset = new Vector3(0, 0.08f, 0); follower.rotationOffset = new Vector3(90, 0, 0);
            var back = Button(wrist.transform, "Back", "", Vector3.zero, new Vector2(0.11f, 0.06f), assets, buttonMaterial, click, warp);
            warp.wrist = wrist; wrist.SetActive(false);
            var voice = client.gameObject.AddComponent<HostVoice>(); voice.connection = connection; voice.world = world; voice.warp = warp;
            voice.source = WorldDrawing.Sound(WorldDrawing.Group("ForemanVoice", client), false, HostVoice.SilentMetres);
            voice.source.minDistance = HostVoice.ClearMetres;
            PlayPanelsBuilder.Build(client, wrist.transform, voice, presenter, warp, xr, sun, assets, dark, buttonMaterial);
            var attention = warpRoot.gameObject.AddComponent<CrewAttention>(); attention.warp = warp; attention.presenter = presenter;
            attention.ringMaterial = assets.soil;

            var preview = Root("Preview"); preview.gameObject.tag = "EditorOnly";
            var survey = WorldDrawing.Group("SurveyCamera", preview).AddComponent<Camera>();
            survey.transform.position = new Vector3(0, 1.65f, 0); survey.clearFlags = CameraClearFlags.SolidColor;
            survey.backgroundColor = Color.clear; survey.allowHDR = false; survey.nearClipPlane = 0.05f; survey.farClipPlane = 6000;
            survey.enabled = false;
            xr.Resolve(false);
            // This owned child lives under Bryan's rig. Reuse it so rebuilding never duplicates it or changes the rig.
            const string HeadCameraName = "[LOBOver] HeadPreviewCamera";
            var head = xr.CentreEye.Find(HeadCameraName);
            var camera = head == null ? WorldDrawing.Group(HeadCameraName, xr.CentreEye).AddComponent<Camera>() : head.GetComponent<Camera>();
            camera.gameObject.tag = "EditorOnly"; camera.clearFlags = CameraClearFlags.SolidColor;
            camera.backgroundColor = Color.clear; camera.allowHDR = false; camera.depth = 100;
            var desktop = preview.gameObject.AddComponent<PlayPreview>(); desktop.view = camera; desktop.survey = survey; desktop.rig = xr; desktop.back = back;
            desktop.AlignHeadCamera();
            xr.editorHead = camera.transform;
            sky.observer = camera.transform;
            sky.rig = xr;
            // Editor inspection shows the complete site; WarpController sets the hub state in Play.
            Shader.SetGlobalFloat("_WarpProgress", 1);
            Shader.SetGlobalFloat("_WarpRadius", WarpController.RestRadius);
            Shader.SetGlobalFloat("_WarpBand", WarpController.BandMetres);
            EditorSceneManager.MarkSceneDirty(scene);
            if (saveScene)
            {
                if (!EditorSceneManager.SaveScene(scene)) throw new IOException("Could not save " + ScenePath);
                EditorBuildSettings.scenes = new[] { new EditorBuildSettingsScene(ScenePath, true) };
            }
            AssetDatabase.SaveAssets();
            Debug.Log("LOB Over: Play Scene built; Sync Data before the Quest build. Start Play LOB Over on the PC.");
            return scene;
        }
        private static Transform Root(string name) => new GameObject("[LOBOver] " + name).transform;
        private static Material Material(string name, string shaderName, Color color, bool required = true)
        {
            var shader = Shader.Find(shaderName);
            if (shader == null) { if (!required) return null; throw new InvalidOperationException("Missing shader " + shaderName); }
            var path = SiteAssetBuilder.Art + name + ".mat";
            var material = AssetDatabase.LoadAssetAtPath<Material>(path);
            if (material == null) { material = new Material(shader); AssetDatabase.CreateAsset(material, path); }
            material.shader = shader;
            if (material.HasProperty("_BaseColor")) material.SetColor("_BaseColor", color);
            EditorUtility.SetDirty(material); return material;
        }
        private static TextMeshPro Label(string name, Transform parent, string value, Vector3 position, Vector2 size, float height, WorldAssets assets)
        {
            var label = WorldDrawing.Text(name, parent, position, size, height, assets.font);
            // TMP uses a tenth of a world unit per font unit. Size the capital glyph, not its line box.
            var face = assets.font.faceInfo;
            label.fontSize = height * 10 * face.pointSize / Mathf.Max(1, face.capLine);
            label.alignment = TextAlignmentOptions.Center; label.text = value; return label;
        }
        private static CrewButton Button(Transform parent, string title, string crew, Vector3 position, Vector2 size,
            WorldAssets assets, Material material, AudioClip click, WarpController warp)
        {
            var obj = WorldDrawing.Group(string.IsNullOrEmpty(crew) ? "Back" : "Visit_" + crew, parent);
            obj.transform.localPosition = position;
            WorldDrawing.Box("Face", obj.transform, Vector3.zero, new Vector3(size.x, size.y, 0.006f), material);
